iety where you can hear sirens going  off you're literally willing to do  whatever you have to do  to get out of that state of mind because  it's so terrifying it's all-encompassing  and it like it's almost suffocating  interesting what what what have you  learned from all of your  debauchery and craziness  you know i didn't learn because you seem  very stable and level-headed right now  you know i didn't i don't think i  started learning until i had kids  you know  um because i think that they gave my  life purpose  so  before it was just  it was like i've been stripped down to  like those primal instincts of just  survival and survival for me had been  mutated to fill this  drug addiction you know it was like part  of my survival just as a normal human  being would have to satiate hunger i had  to satiate this particular appetite once  i had kids i learned that it wasn't  about me anymore you know  i was secondary and my obligation as a  human being was to provide as a parent  not just financially but morally as well  so when you talk about a moral compass  being skewed or going away entirely once  i had children i started being  conscientious of that moral compass  again and it kind of gave me direction  it was a beacon towards  a moral standpoint that i wanted to  achieve  to be a good example to my kids  and that's ultimately  what was transformative for me that's  what that's what finally clicked  was having to  be held accountable by by kids that are  essentially blameless in the entire  thing you know i want to stop the cycle  right now  it's great to hear  awesome ryan thank you so much for  sharing your story you're welcome thanks  for having me that's great thank you  you
 

